//? Title: Greedy Best-First Search
//? Subtitle: Expanding whatever looks closest to the goal: why it is fast, when it is wasteful, the high-water mark that predicts its work, and its modern use as the search loop of graph-based nearest-neighbour indexes, on the volcano terrain and the handwritten digits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Greedy best-first search (GBFS) always expands the vertex whose heuristic \(h\) says it is
closest to the goal, ignoring the cost already paid. We prove that with the perfect heuristic
\(h^*\) it expands only the cells of the path it returns, and that the path can still be
suboptimal. We also derive the *high-water mark* bounds: before reaching the goal, GBFS must
flood every heuristic depression it starts in. On the volcano terrain, GBFS walks straight to
the goal, expanding 76 cells against A*'s 1,732, but pays 17% more. With the perfect heuristic
it pays 22% more, because it takes the steps that lower \(h^*\) fastest, not the cheapest. On a
10-nearest-neighbour graph of the handwritten digits, best-first search with a result list of
size \(e\!f\) is the search loop of HNSW-style vector indexes. Greedy routing (\(e\!f=1\)) stops in a
local minimum for 271 of 297 queries. The depressions it falls into hold 16 vertices at the
median, and the escape bounds hold for every query. With \(e\!f=128\) the graph search finds the
exact neighbour 99.7% of the time from 409 distance computations, beating a k-d tree at high
recall but not at low budgets. New library pieces are `algorithms::terrain_best_first`,
`algorithms::high_water_mark` and `geometry::proximity_graph_search`.
)

\toc

> The algorithm

Best-first search keeps an *open list* of discovered but unexpanded vertices and always expands
the one with the smallest evaluation \(f\). *Greedy* best-first search (GBFS) uses the heuristic
alone, \(f(v)=h(v)\), an estimate of the remaining cost to the goal, and ignores the cost
\(g(v)\) already paid to reach \(v\). A* uses \(f=g+h\). Writing \(f=\alpha g+h\) puts both on one scale:
\(\alpha=0\) is greedy and \(\alpha=1\) is A*. `algorithms::terrain_best_first` takes \(\alpha\) and any
table of heuristic values.

```
GreedyBestFirst(start, goal, h):
    open = {start};  closed = {}
    while open is not empty:
        u = the vertex in open with the smallest h(u);  move u from open to closed
        if u == goal: return the path to u through the parent pointers
        for each neighbour v of u not in closed:
            if v is new, or g(u) + c(u, v) < g(v):  g(v) = g(u) + c(u, v);  parent(v) = u;  add v to open
    return failure
```

The closed list makes GBFS complete on a finite graph. Each vertex is expanded at most once, so
it always finds a path if one exists. But nothing makes the path cheap, and nothing bounds the
work below "the whole graph". Both depend entirely on how \(h\) is shaped.

>> With a perfect heuristic

Let \(h=h^*\), the exact cost to go. If \(u\) is expanded and \(u'\) is its successor on an optimal path,
then \(h^*(u')=h^*(u)-c(u,u')<h^*(u)\). Every vertex still in the open list was generated before
\(u\) was chosen, so it has \(h^*\ge h^*(u)>h^*(u')\). The next vertex expanded is therefore a
neighbour of \(u\) with smaller \(h^*\) than \(u\). The expansions form a single chain with strictly
decreasing \(h^*\), ending at the goal. GBFS with \(h^*\) **expands exactly the cells of the path it
returns, and nothing else**.

The path need not be optimal, though. GBFS steps to the neighbour \(m\) with the smallest \(h^*(m)\),
not the smallest \(c(u,m)+h^*(m)\). Its total cost exceeds the optimum by the sum of the per-step
excesses:

$$
\operatorname{cost}-C^*=\sum_{i}\Big(c(u_i,u_{i+1})+h^*(u_{i+1})-h^*(u_i)\Big)\;\ge\;0 ,
$$
\alttext(The cost minus C star equals the sum over steps i of c of u i, u i plus 1, plus h star of u i plus 1, minus h star of u i. This is at least 0.)

where each term vanishes exactly when the step lies on an optimal path from \(u_i\). A step can be
expensive and still lower \(h^*\) the most, for example a steep climb straight towards the goal.

>> The high-water mark

With a weaker \(h\), GBFS can wander. Wilt and Ruml (2014) gave a sharp description of how far.
Define the *high-water mark*

$$
H=\min_{\text{paths }P:\,s\to t}\;\max_{v\in P}h(v),
$$
\alttext(H equals the minimum over paths P from s to t of the maximum over vertices v in P of h of v.)

the lowest "water level" at which some start–goal path stays dry. Then:

- **GBFS never expands a vertex with \(h>H\).** Some path keeps \(h\le H\) all the way. Until the
  goal is expanded, one of its vertices is always in the open list, and it is preferred to any
  vertex with \(h>H\).
- **GBFS expands every vertex reachable from the start through vertices with \(h<H\).** Call this
  region \(R\). The goal is not in \(R\), or \(H\) would be smaller. While part of \(R\) is unexpanded, the
  open list holds a vertex of \(R\) with \(h<H\), and it is preferred to every vertex with \(h\ge H\),
  the goal included.

So \(|R|\le\) expansions \(\le|\{v\text{ reachable through }h\le H\}|\). A *heuristic depression* (a
basin where \(h\) is low but the goal cannot be reached without climbing out) must be flooded in
full before GBFS can leave it. `algorithms::high_water_mark` computes \(H\) by a minimax
version of Dijkstra's algorithm and returns both region sizes. Since every path includes the
start, \(H\ge h(s)\), and \(R\) is non-empty only when the start itself lies in a depression.

> On the volcano

We reuse the walking problem of the A* and beam-search reports. `dm::datasets::volcano()` gives
Maunga Whau's elevation on a 10 m grid. A step costs its horizontal length plus 8.3 m for each
metre climbed (Naismith's rule), and the walk goes from the south-east foot of the cone to the
north-west corner. The admissible heuristic `OctileClimb` is the 8-neighbour distance plus 8.3
times the net height still to gain. The perfect heuristic \(h^*\) comes from one backward Dijkstra
run (`terrain_cost_to_go`). Between them we blend

$$
h_\lambda=(1-\lambda)\,h_{\text{octile}}+\lambda\,h^*,\qquad 0\le\lambda\le1 ,
$$
\alttext(h lambda equals 1 minus lambda times h octile, plus lambda times h star, for lambda between 0 and 1.)

which stays admissible and consistent, since both ends are. We run GBFS and A* for each \(\lambda\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/best_first_terrain.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", 5000.0 / 600.0);
  const std::size_t start = 80 * grid.cols + 5, goal = 5 * grid.cols + 55, cells = grid.elevation.size();
  const auto hstar = alg::terrain_cost_to_go(grid, goal);
  std::vector<double> octile(cells);
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < cells; ++c)
    octile[c] = alg::terrain_heuristic(grid, c, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb);
  const double optimum = hstar[start];
  std::print("Grid of {} cells. Optimal cost \\(C^*={:.1f}\\).\n\n", cells, optimum);

  std::print("| \\(\\lambda\\) | GBFS expansions | GBFS path cells | GBFS cost / \\(C^*\\) | A* expansions | A* cost / \\(C^*\\) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lam, greedy_work, astar_work, greedy_cost;
  alg::TerrainPath greedy0, astar0;
  std::vector<std::size_t> region0;
  for (double lambda : {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9, 0.95, 1.0}) {
    std::vector<double> h(cells);
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < cells; ++c) h[c] = (1 - lambda) * octile[c] + lambda * hstar[c];
    const auto greedy = alg::terrain_best_first(grid, start, goal, h, 0.0);
    const auto astar = alg::terrain_best_first(grid, start, goal, h, 1.0);
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {} | {} | {:.3f} | {} | {:.3f} |\n", lambda, greedy.expansion_order.size(),
               greedy.path.size(), greedy.cost / optimum, astar.expansion_order.size(), astar.cost / optimum);
    lam.push_back(lambda);
    greedy_work.push_back(std::log10(greedy.expansion_order.size()));
    astar_work.push_back(std::log10(astar.expansion_order.size()));
    greedy_cost.push_back(greedy.cost / optimum);
    if (lambda == 0.0) {
      greedy0 = greedy;
      astar0 = astar;
    }
  }

  // Maps of the octile-heuristic runs.
  const auto coords = [&](const std::vector<std::size_t>& list) {
    std::pair<std::vector<double>, std::vector<double>> xy;
    for (const std::size_t c : list) {
      xy.first.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
      xy.second.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
    }
    return xy;
  };
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < cells; ++c) z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = grid.elevation[c];
  const auto best = alg::terrain_a_star(grid, start, goal, alg::TerrainHeuristic::OctileClimb);
  const auto [ox, oy] = coords(best.path);
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (const auto* run : {&greedy0, &astar0}) {
    const auto [ex, ey] = coords(run->expansion_order);
    const auto [px, py] = coords(run->path);
    layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(20))
                   .points(ex, ey, "", dm::RGB{30, 64, 175}, 2.0)
                   .lines(ox, oy, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.5)
                   .lines(px, py, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.5)
                   .title(std::format("{}: {} cells expanded, cost {:+.1f}%", run == &greedy0 ? "Greedy" : "A*",
                                      run->expansion_order.size(), 100.0 * (run->cost / optimum - 1.0)))
                   .x_label("x (m)")
                   .y_label("y (m)"));
  }
  layout.size(1200, 470).save_svg("build/best_first_terrain.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Grid of 5307 cells. Optimal cost \(C^*=1291.5\).

| \(\lambda\) | GBFS expansions | GBFS path cells | GBFS cost / \(C^*\) | A* expansions | A* cost / \(C^*\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.00 | 76 | 76 | 1.167 | 1732 | 1.000 |
| 0.25 | 76 | 76 | 1.167 | 1315 | 1.000 |
| 0.50 | 76 | 76 | 1.193 | 813 | 1.000 |
| 0.75 | 76 | 76 | 1.199 | 522 | 1.000 |
| 0.90 | 83 | 83 | 1.224 | 312 | 1.000 |
| 0.95 | 83 | 83 | 1.224 | 229 | 1.000 |
| 1.00 | 83 | 83 | 1.224 | 150 | 1.000 |
\image(figures/best_first_terrain.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Cells expanded (blue) with the octile-climb heuristic by greedy best-first search (left) and A* (right) on the volcano. Red is the path each returns, black the optimal path.)
\alttext(Two maps of the volcano coloured by elevation, 860 by 600 metres. Left, greedy: a single red line of 76 expanded cells runs diagonally from the start at the bottom right (800, 50) up to y equals 550 at x equals 300, crossing the south-east shoulder of the cone, then west to the goal at (50, 550); the black optimal path instead runs north up the east edge and west along the top. Right, A*: blue dots fill the eastern third of the map and a band along the top, and the red path coincides with the black optimal one.)

Greedy best-first search never backtracks on this terrain. With the octile heuristic it expands
76 cells and returns a path of exactly those 76 cells, for every \(\lambda\) up to 0.75. With
\(\lambda\ge0.9\) it is 83 and 83. The octile-climb heuristic has no depressions here: from every
cell some neighbour lowers \(h\). The map shows the cost of that single-mindedness. GBFS heads
diagonally for the goal, climbs over the cone's south-eastern shoulder and pays 16.7% more than
the optimum. The optimal path instead walks north along the low eastern edge.

A better heuristic makes greedy search *worse* here: with \(h_{0.5}\) the excess is 19.3%, and with
the perfect \(h^*\) it is 22.4%. This is the per-step excess derived above at work. \(h^*\) falls
fastest along steep climbs towards the goal, which are expensive, while the cheap route first
moves sideways. The same heuristics make A* steadily better. It always returns \(C^*\), and its
expansions fall from 1,732 with the octile heuristic to 150 with \(h^*\), close to the 99 cells
of an optimal path. GBFS uses \(h\) to choose a direction, A* to prune, and only pruning benefits
from accuracy.

> Best-first search on a proximity graph

Greedy best-first search has a second life as the search loop of the graph indexes behind
modern vector databases (navigable small-world graphs and HNSW, Malkov et al. 2014, Malkov and
Yashunin 2018). The points are vertices, edges join near neighbours, and the heuristic is the
distance to the query, \(h(v)=\|v-q\|\). Here the goal is unknown: it is whichever vertex
minimises \(h\). So the stopping rule changes. A result list keeps the \(e\!f\) closest vertices
seen, and the search stops when the closest unexpanded vertex is farther than all of them.
With \(e\!f=1\) this is *greedy routing*: move to the closest neighbour until none is closer. It stops
at the first local minimum of \(h\), a heuristic depression of size one. A larger \(e\!f\)
keeps more of the open list alive and climbs out of shallow depressions. `geometry::proximity_graph_search`
implements this loop.

We build a symmetric 10-nearest-neighbour graph on the same 1,500 indexed digits as the Best Bin
First report (`stats::KnnGraph`). We search it for the 297 query digits from vertex 0, and compare
recall and distance computations with Best Bin First's k-d tree.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/best_first_graph.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <numeric>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::size_t rows = digits.nrows(), train = 1500;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> x(rows, std::vector<double>(64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto column = digits.get_column("pixel_" + std::to_string(j)).values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i) x[i][j] = column[i];
  }
  const auto label = digits.get_column("digit").values();
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> index(x.begin(), x.begin() + train);
  const dm::stats::KnnGraph graph(index, 10);
  std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>> adjacency(train);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < train; ++i) adjacency[i] = graph.neighbors(i);
  std::print("Graph: {} vertices, {} edges (mean degree {:.1f}), {} connected component(s).\n\n", train, graph.edges(),
             2.0 * graph.edges() / train, graph.components());

  std::vector<int> truth;
  for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) truth.push_back(geo::knn_brute_force(index, x[q], 1)[0]);
  const double nq = static_cast<double>(rows - train);

  std::print("| search | setting | distance computations | expansions | recall | 1-NN accuracy |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> g_eval, g_recall, k_eval, k_recall;
  double greedy_hops_gap = 0;
  for (int ef : {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}) {
    double evals = 0, expansions = 0, recall = 0, right = 0;
    for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) {
      const auto hit = geo::proximity_graph_search(index, adjacency, x[q], 0, 1, ef);
      evals += hit.evaluations;
      expansions += hit.expansions;
      recall += hit.indices[0] == truth[q - train];
      right += label[hit.indices[0]] == label[q];
      if (ef == 1) {
        double best = 0;
        for (int a = 0; a < 64; ++a) best += (index[truth[q - train]][a] - x[q][a]) * (index[truth[q - train]][a] - x[q][a]);
        greedy_hops_gap += hit.distances[0] / std::max(std::sqrt(best), 1e-12);
      }
    }
    std::print("| graph best-first | \\(e\\!f={}\\) | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% |\n", ef, evals / nq, expansions / nq,
               recall / nq, 100 * right / nq);
    g_eval.push_back(std::log2(evals / nq));
    g_recall.push_back(recall / nq);
  }
  const geo::BestBinFirstKd tree(index);
  for (int budget : {16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024}) {
    double evals = 0, recall = 0, right = 0;
    for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) {
      const auto hit = tree.knn(x[q], 1, budget);
      evals += hit.examined;
      recall += hit.indices[0] == truth[q - train];
      right += label[hit.indices[0]] == label[q];
    }
    std::print("| k-d tree, Best Bin First | budget {} | {:.0f} | – | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% |\n", budget, evals / nq,
               recall / nq, 100 * right / nq);
    k_eval.push_back(std::log2(evals / nq));
    k_recall.push_back(recall / nq);
  }
  std::print("\nGreedy routing (\\(e\\!f=1\\)) ends on average {:.3f} times as far from the query as the true nearest "
             "neighbour.\n", greedy_hops_gap / nq);

  // The depressions where greedy routing stops: greedy best-first search from the stuck vertex m
  // to the true neighbour, with h = distance to the query, against the high-water bounds.
  std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>> edges;
  for (std::size_t u = 0; u < train; ++u)
    for (const std::size_t v : adjacency[u])
      if (u < v) edges.emplace_back(u, v);
  std::vector<double> region, flood, work;
  int bracketed = 0;
  for (std::size_t q = train; q < rows; ++q) {
    const auto stuck = static_cast<std::size_t>(geo::proximity_graph_search(index, adjacency, x[q], 0, 1, 1).indices[0]);
    const auto target = static_cast<std::size_t>(truth[q - train]);
    if (stuck == target) continue;
    std::vector<double> h(train);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < train; ++i) {
      double s = 0;
      for (int a = 0; a < 64; ++a) s += (index[i][a] - x[q][a]) * (index[i][a] - x[q][a]);
      h[i] = std::sqrt(s);
    }
    const auto mark = dm::algorithms::high_water_mark(train, edges, stuck, target, h, false);
    const auto run = dm::algorithms::best_first_search(train, edges, stuck, target, h, false);
    region.push_back(mark.below);
    flood.push_back(mark.at_most);
    work.push_back(run.order.size());
    bracketed += mark.below <= run.order.size() && run.order.size() <= mark.at_most + 1;
  }
  const auto median = [](std::vector<double> v) {
    std::ranges::sort(v);
    return v[v.size() / 2];
  };
  const auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& v) { return std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0.0) / v.size(); };
  std::print("\nGreedy routing stopped at a local minimum \\(m\\) other than the nearest neighbour for {} of {} queries. "
             "From \\(m\\), greedy best-first search towards the true neighbour:\n\n", work.size(), rows - train);
  std::print("| | median | mean |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| depression \\(|R(m)|\\): reachable from \\(m\\) through \\(h<H\\) | {:.0f} | {:.1f} |\n", median(region), mean(region));
  std::print("| GBFS expansions from \\(m\\) | {:.0f} | {:.1f} |\n", median(work), mean(work));
  std::print("| upper bound: reachable through \\(h\\le H\\) | {:.0f} | {:.1f} |\n", median(flood), mean(flood));
  std::print("\nExpansions inside the bounds \\(|R|\\le\\) expansions \\(\\le|R_\\le|+1\\) for {} of {} queries.\n", bracketed, work.size());

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(g_eval, g_recall, "graph best-first", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
      .points(g_eval, g_recall, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .lines(k_eval, k_recall, "k-d tree BBF", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 2.5)
      .points(k_eval, k_recall, "", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 6.0)
      .y_limits(0, 1.2)
      .title("Recall against work on the digits")
      .x_label("log2 distance computations per query")
      .y_label("recall of the nearest neighbour")
      .size(820, 430)
      .save_svg("build/best_first_graph.svg");
}
```
