Greedy routing stopped at a local minimum \(m\) other than the nearest neighbour for 271 of 297 queries. From \(m\), greedy best-first search towards the true neighbour:

| | median | mean |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| depression \(|R(m)|\): reachable from \(m\) through \(h<H\) | 16 | 24.2 |
| GBFS expansions from \(m\) | 36 | 41.3 |
| upper bound: reachable through \(h\le H\) | 532 | 557.8 |

Expansions inside the bounds \(|R|\le\) expansions \(\le|R_\le|+1\) for 271 of 271 queries.
\image(figures/best_first_graph.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Recall of the exact nearest neighbour against the mean number of distance computations per query, for best-first search on a 10-NN graph (blue, \(e\!f\) from 1 to 128) and Best Bin First on a k-d tree (orange, budget 16 to 1,024).)
\alttext(Line chart of recall against log2 distance computations per query. The k-d tree curve in orange rises steadily from 0.26 at 16 computations to 0.99 at about 1,000. The graph best-first curve in blue starts lower, at 0.09 with about 36 computations, stays below the k-d tree until about 256 computations, then rises steeply to 0.93 at 289 and 1.0 at 409, above the k-d tree.)

The graph is connected, and each digit has 13.5 neighbours on average. Greedy routing from vertex 0
costs 36 distance computations and finds the exact nearest neighbour for only 8.8% of queries.
On average it stops 2.3 times as far from the query as the true neighbour. Only 10.1% of its
answers have the right digit, which is chance level: after about three hops it is still near
the entry vertex. It stops in a local minimum of \(h\), a vertex all of whose
neighbours are farther from the query. From such a vertex \(m\), GBFS towards the true neighbour
has to flood the depression \(R(m)\) first: 16 vertices at the median and 24 on average. It then
spends about as much again on the far side, 36 expansions at the median in total. The high-water
bounds hold for all 271 stuck queries. The upper bound is loose (532 at the median), because once
over the barrier, the goal's own basin of low \(h\) is large.

Raising \(e\!f\) lets the search climb out of depressions. Recall reaches 0.66 at \(e\!f=32\), 0.93 at 64 and
0.997 at 128, and 1-NN accuracy reaches 94.3%, the exact value being 94.6%. Against Best Bin
First the picture depends on the budget. Below about 250 distance computations the k-d tree is
better: the graph search spends its first 30 or so computations just walking from vertex 0
towards the query's region. Above that budget the graph wins clearly, with 0.997 recall from 409
computations against the k-d tree's 0.936 from 510. HNSW addresses the entry-point cost with a
hierarchy of sparser graphs that brings the search close to the query in a few long hops.

> Summary

- Greedy best-first search orders the open list by \(h\) alone. It is complete with a closed list,
  but neither its cost nor its work is bounded.
- With the perfect heuristic, its expansions are exactly its path. Its excess cost is
  \(\sum_i(c(u_i,u_{i+1})+h^*(u_{i+1})-h^*(u_i))\), which a step can make positive by lowering
  \(h^*\) quickly at high cost. On the volcano, \(h^*\) made GBFS 22% worse than optimal, against
  17% with the octile heuristic, while it cut A*'s work from 1,732 cells to 150.
- The high-water mark \(H=\min_P\max_{v\in P}h(v)\) brackets GBFS. It expands every vertex reachable
  through \(h<H\) and none with \(h>H\). Heuristic depressions must be flooded in full.
- On a 10-NN graph of the digits, greedy routing gets stuck in a depression for 91% of queries.
  Best-first search with a result list of 128 finds the exact neighbour 99.7% of the time from
  409 distance computations, better than a k-d tree at high recall. Its weakness is the walk from
  a fixed entry point, which HNSW's hierarchy removes.

